{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1515687466,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1Njk3ODQ1Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2068#issuecomment-356978452"},"message":"A timely issue, thanks @kskyten  ;)  \nIndeed, while thinking about further `run` development we are considering to extend it to support for arbitrary (list of) \"executioners\" (currently we just call those commands using subprocess.Popen).  In a brief car ride with @kyleam we even considered for those to report back to the datalad a list of *input* files within the dataset which were used to produce the output (modified/new) files, which would allow to seamlessly establish the full provenance graph, which we could indeed pass on to other tools such as Vistrails.  And that is where `reprozip` or our, in parts inspired by reprozip, [NICEMAN](https://github.com/ReproNim/niceman) could be used to either provide additional provenance information, or generate detailed descriptions of the environment (that is what we are trying to achieve currently with the `retrace` command of the niceman), or even the images of the environment (e.g., from reprozip).\n\nWe are aware of the Sumatra project since it has \"neural computation\" roots, although personally I haven't used it.  We should indeed review how it could fit into the picture as well, or which aspects/usecases we could address/borrow.\n\nBut overall point is that with datalad we (or at least I) do not want to reinvent the entire wheel of the workflow engine or alike.  I think we should just capitalize on the information which we already have and possibly make it possible to gather even more information which could (later) come useful. Git allows us to establish provenance of the results natively, and git-annex provides the facility to handle binary artefacts/data efficiently while keeping info about provenance of individual files. So we have lots of \"provenance\" pieces already spread around (git history, git-annex) and the idea is to make good use of it. Even the simple `run` command we have already shown to be quite useful.  It, together with `git annex addurl` replaced my custom \"run\" bash alias which I used to record locally the commands I was interested to \"record\" for future reference. If we could enhance its utility by interfacing to additional frameworks, which concentrate on specific aspects of reproducibility (such as reprozip, niceman, etc), it would be great. And probably first we should compile the list of those, and a list of usecases we would like to address.  E.g. @kyleam is looking ATM into a first step of extending `run` to be able to simply rerun those commands which were recorded in the git history (not just the last one).  That simple functionality could already help to easily answer questions such as \"do results change if I (re)run computation in the same or different environment\".  And one aspect coming into mind -- investigation of differences.  That is where we or someone else could (later) provide support for various \"diffing\" mechanisms which would be domain/application specific. And that goes along the ideas of the [testkraut](https://testkraut.readthedocs.io) project.\n\nedit 1: finally remembered one more project related https://github.com/gems-uff/noworkflow","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1515765432,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1NzI0NDY1MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2068#issuecomment-357244650"},"message":"If we add such capabilities, we should also add the ability to run a command in a specified container (e.g. singularity) -- this will achieve the same goal (comprehensive description), albeit in a different way. We could simply annex the container image to achieve a comparable level of reproducibility.","files":null}]}